WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:06.340
A stolen password shouldn't be enough to get
into your tenant.

2
00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:11.830
With a single conditional access policy, every
sign-in you choose has to prove it's really

3
00:00:11.890 --> 00:00:15.200
the user before it's let near your resources.

4
00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:19.020
Here's how you build that in a few minutes.
You'll do four things.

5
00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:24.220
Create a conditional access policy, point it at
a test group of users, require

6
00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:28.040
multi-factor authentication, and switch it on.

7
00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:31.440
So let's open the Microsoft Entra Admin Center.

8
00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:36.920
First, what conditional access is. Think of it
as an if-then rule that sits on

9
00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:42.200
the sign-in. If a sign-in matches the conditions
you set, these users, this app,

10
00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:47.000
then Entra steps in and asks for more proof
before it grants access.

11
00:00:47.030 --> 00:00:50.440
That's why it's the recommended way to require
MFA.

12
00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:54.110
You decide who gets prompted and when. So let's
build one.

13
00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:59.490
In the Admin Center, browse to Entra ID, then
Conditional Access, and select

14
00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:04.200
New Policy. Give it a clear name like MFA Pilot.

15
00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:09.540
Now the who, under Assignments, Open Users,
choose your test group and

16
00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:14.960
select it. The policy targets just those users
so no one else is affected while you

17
00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:19.360
test. Next, the what, which sign-ins trigger the
rule.

18
00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:23.240
Under Cloud Apps or Actions, choose Select
Resources.

19
00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:28.280
For this walkthrough, pick the Windows Azure
Service Management API, then

20
00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:33.320
choose Select. That scopes the policy to
sign-ins for Azure management.

21
00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:38.260
With the who and the what set, there's one thing
left, the requirement

22
00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:43.750
itself. This is the part that matters. Under
Access Controls, open Grant

23
00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:48.730
and select Grant Access. Then check Require
Multi-Factor Authentication

24
00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:54.070
and choose Select. You've just told Entra that
for these users, on this

25
00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:58.830
app, a password alone isn't enough. They also
have to pass a second

26
00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:03.080
factor. The policy is built, but it isn't live
yet.

27
00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:08.240
Scroll to Enable Policy and flip it to on. You
could set it to report only first to

28
00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:12.440
preview the impact, but for a small test group,
turn it straight on.

29
00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:16.570
Then select Create and the policy is active.

30
00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:22.050
So four steps. You created a conditional access
policy, aimed it at a test

31
00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:26.440
group, required multi-factor authentication, and
turned it on.

32
00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:31.840
Now, when those users sign into that app, a
password only gets them part way.

33
00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:35.880
Then Entra asks for the second factor before it
lets them through.

34
00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:40.720
Try it with a test user and watch the MFA prompt
appear on the next sign-in.

35
00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:45.810
When you're ready to go further, the next
tutorial builds a risk-based conditional access

36
00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:50.360
policy, one that asks for MFA only when a
sign-in looks risky.

37
00:02:50.390 --> 00:02:52.020
Find it on Microsoft Learn.
